//# Calendar dates, times, civil periods, machine-time projections, and
//# IANA-zone-aware date-time arithmetic.
//# A date or time reads as a reader of a locale writes it with `format`, at
//# a style's length (`Date.format(d, locale, style: .Long)`) or with the
//# fields a skeleton names (`Date.format_fields(d, locale,
//# DateFields"yMMMd")`), and is read back from what a reader typed with
//# `parse_in`. A fixed numeric pattern, the same in every locale, is for
//# file names and protocols: `Date.to_string_with(d, DatePattern"yyyyMMdd")`
//# and `parse_with`. `TimeUnit.format_relative` writes "in 3 days" or
//# "yesterday". The calendar is the Gregorian one; the words and patterns
//# come from CLDR, from the data the program carries.
//#
//# `std::calendar` covers civil dates and times: wall-clock readings, zoned
//# moments, the conversions between them, and DST-aware arithmetic.
//# `std::instant` has the clock and the absolute `Instant`, and
//# `std::duration` has exact elapsed time, `Duration`.
//#
//# ## The type ladder
//#
//# Five civil types, in increasing specificity:
//#
//# - **`Date`**: a calendar day. Year, month and day; no time of day.
//# - **`Time`**: a time of day. Hour, minute, second and nanosecond; no
//#   calendar.
//# - **`NaiveDateTime`**: a wall-clock reading. A date and a time of day, no
//#   zone. It names no moment.
//# - **`OffsetDateTime`**: anchored at a fixed UTC offset. A real moment; no
//#   DST.
//# - **`DateTime`**: anchored in an IANA zone (`America/New_York`,
//#   `Asia/Tokyo`, ...), following its daylight-saving rules. The type to
//#   use for a moment.
//#
//# Two adjacent types live in sibling modules:
//#
//# - **`Instant`** (`std::instant`): nanoseconds since the Unix epoch, in
//#   UTC. It has no calendar and no zone. `Instant + Duration`
//#   and `Instant - Duration` are Instants; `a - b` on two Instants is the
//#   `Duration` from `b` to `a` (`Instant.between(b, a)`).
//# - **`Duration`** (`std::duration`): exact elapsed time, a count of
//#   nanoseconds. Build one with a unit function such as
//#   `Duration.seconds(5)`, or with a literal as `Display` writes it,
//#   `Duration"1h30m"`. Hours, minutes and seconds are units there; months
//#   and years are not, since their lengths vary.
//#
//# The five types mirror `java.time` (`LocalDate`, `LocalTime`,
//# `LocalDateTime`, `OffsetDateTime`, `ZonedDateTime`) under other names.
//# The bare name `DateTime` is the zoned type, the one to use for a moment.
//# The reading with no zone has the longer name, `NaiveDateTime`, so code
//# that uses it says that the value names no moment.
//#
//# ## Construction and typed literals
//#
//# Each of the five types has a typed literal, the usual way to write a
//# value:
//#
//# ```text
//# Date"YYYY-MM-DD"
//# Time"HH:MM:SS[.fff…]"
//# NaiveDateTime"YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS[.fff…]"
//# OffsetDateTime"YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS[.fff…]±HH:MM"  // or trailing Z
//# DateTime"YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS[.fff…]±HH:MM[Zone/Name]"
//# ```
//#
//# A literal without slots (`Date"2026-05-04"`)
//# is parsed when the program compiles: it is a `Date`, and a malformed one
//# is a compile error. A literal with a slot (`Date"2026-${month}-04"`) is
//# parsed at run time, and its parse failure returns `Err(calendar.Error)`;
//# unwrap it with `?` at the call site. `Date.parse` reads text the program
//# gets while it runs. The spec's
//# [typed literals](/spec/typed-literals-and-keyword-declarations/) give the
//# mechanism.
//#
//# `DateTime`'s literal uses RFC 9557 bracket notation, and the IANA zone
//# bracket is required. A bare offset (no bracket) parses as
//# `OffsetDateTime"…"` instead: the bracket tells "anchored in a zone"
//# from "anchored at a fixed offset".
//#
//# The programmatic constructors are `Date.new`, `Time.new` and
//# `NaiveDateTime.new` (validated, each a `Result`), `DateTime.in_zone` (a
//# wall reading projected through a zone, below),
//# `OffsetDateTime.with_offset`, `OffsetDateTime.from_instant` (each a `Result`),
//# `DateTime.from_instant_in` (a `Result`: `UnknownZone`), `DateTime.now_in`
//# (the system clock projected into a zone), `DateTime.now_local` (the same
//# in the zone the machine is set to, which `DateTime.local_zone` names) and
//# `NaiveDateTime.utc_now` (the system clock as a UTC wall reading).
//#
//# ## Years of any size
//#
//# The calendar is the proleptic Gregorian one in both directions, with no
//# first or last year: a year is an `Int` of any size, and no construction,
//# conversion or arithmetic fails or faults for how far it goes. A year from
//# 0000 to 9999 is written with four digits, and any other with a sign and
//# at least six, ISO 8601's expanded form as RFC 9557 writes it:
//# `Date"+010000-01-01"`, `Date"-000044-03-15"` (45 BC, since year 0 is
//# 1 BC). Every `parse` reads that form back, and also a year of more than
//# four digits without a sign (`10000-01-01`) and a sign with four
//# (`-0044-03-15`).
//#
//# ```nomi
//# Date"2026-05-04" + Years(300_000_000_000) // → +300000002026-05-04
//# ```
//#
//# ## Arithmetic: `Duration` and civil periods
//#
//# Arithmetic has two intents, and they differ because `+ Hours(24)` is not
//# always `+ Duration.hours(24)` around a DST transition.
//#
//# - **`value + Duration`** and **`value - Duration`** are exact elapsed
//#   time. Adding 24 real hours shifts the instant by 24 hours of physical
//#   time, wherever the wall clock lands. Each time-bearing type implements
//#   `Add` and `Subtract` with `Duration` as the right-hand operand and
//#   itself as the result (`impl Add<Duration, DateTime> for DateTime`).
//# - **`value + Years(...)`** and `Months`, `Weeks`, `Days`, `Hours`,
//#   `Minutes`, `Seconds`, `Milliseconds`, `Microseconds` and `Nanoseconds`
//#   (and `-` of each) are civil period arithmetic. They adjust the wall
//#   reading and, for a zoned `DateTime`, resolve it through the zone again.
//#   Months and years clamp to the end of the month (`Date"2026-01-31" +
//#   Months(1)` is `Date"2026-02-28"`). A `Date` takes years, months, weeks
//#   and days; the date-time types take all ten; a `Time` takes only a
//#   `Duration`, and wraps at midnight.
//#
//# Across a spring-forward transition the two differ:
//#
//# ```nomi
//# start = DateTime"2026-03-07T12:00:00-05:00[America/New_York]"
//#
//# start + Hours(24) // → noon Mar 8 EDT (23h physical)
//#
//# start + Duration.hours(24) // → 1pm  Mar 8 EDT (24h physical)
//# ```
//#
//# The wall reading "tomorrow at noon" stays at noon after the jump, and
//# only 23 hours of real time pass. "Add 24 real hours" advances through
//# the transition and lands at 1pm. Both are correct; the split lets the
//# caller say which intent applies.
//#
//# ## DST: gaps, folds and `Disambiguation`
//#
//# Projecting a wall reading into an IANA zone (`DateTime.in_zone`) can hit
//# one of two anomalies:
//#
//# - **A spring-forward gap**: the wall reading never exists.
//#   `2026-03-08T02:30` in New York is in the gap: 02:00 EST jumps straight
//#   to 03:00 EDT, and the half hour between is unreachable.
//# - **A fall-back fold**: the wall reading exists twice.
//#   `2026-11-01T01:30` in New York happens twice: 02:00 EDT rolls back to
//#   01:00 EST, so 01:30 occurs once before the rollback and once after.
//#
//# The `Disambiguation` enum says how `in_zone` resolves them:
//#
//# | Mode | In a fold | In a gap |
//# |---|---|---|
//# | `Compatible` (default) | Earlier instant (pre-transition) | Post-transition instant |
//# | `Earlier` | Earlier instant | Pre-transition instant |
//# | `Later` | Later instant | Post-transition instant |
//# | `Reject` | `Err(Ambiguous ...)` | `Err(Nonexistent ...)` |
//#
//# `Compatible` is total and deterministic. `Reject` is the strict mode for
//# a wall reading that came from user input (a form, an API payload), where
//# the right answer is to hand the ambiguity back to the user. Civil shifts
//# (`+ Years(...)`, `+ Months(...)`, `+ Hours(...)`, ...) always use
//# `Compatible` internally, so they are total; only
//# `DateTime.in_zone(..., Disambiguation.Reject)` reports a gap or a fold.
//#
//# ## The conversion lattice
//#
//# ```text
//#                                 Date  ──────┐
//#                                             │
//#                                 Time  ──────┤  of / at_midnight
//#                                             ▼
//# Instant  ◄─────────────►  OffsetDateTime ◄────►  NaiveDateTime
//#    │                            ▲                    │
//#    │                            │ to_offset          │ in_zone
//#    │                            │ (lossy)            │ (with mode)
//#    ▼                            │                    ▼
//# Duration                        └──────  DateTime  ◄─┘
//#                                             │
//#                                             └─ with_zone
//#                                                (same instant,
//#                                                 different zone)
//# ```
//#
//# A wall reading is not a moment, so a `NaiveDateTime` becomes one only
//# where the code says whose clock showed it: a zone, with
//# `DateTime.in_zone(naive, zone, mode)`, or a fixed offset, with
//# `OffsetDateTime.with_offset(naive, offset)` or
//# `NaiveDateTime.to_instant(naive, offset)`. `NaiveDateTime.to_instant`
//# with no offset reads the value as a UTC reading, which is what
//# `NaiveDateTime.utc_now` returns.
//#
//# Two conversions are missing on purpose:
//#
//# 1. **`OffsetDateTime` to `DateTime`.** A fixed offset does not determine
//#    an IANA zone: offset `-05:00` is in effect in many zones at various
//#    points in history. The caller names the zone:
//#    `OffsetDateTime.to_instant`, then `DateTime.from_instant_in(instant,
//#    zone)`.
//# 2. **`Date` or `Time` to `Instant`.** A calendar day or a time of day is
//#    not a moment. `NaiveDateTime.of(date, time)` makes a `NaiveDateTime`,
//#    and `in_zone` carries it the rest of the way.
//#
//# ## Equality and ordering
//#
//# `OffsetDateTime` and `DateTime` compare and order by their instant only.
//# Two values with the same underlying nanoseconds and different offsets or
//# zones are equal:
//#
//# ```nomi
//# ny = DateTime"2026-06-15T12:00:00-04:00[America/New_York]"
//#
//# paris = DateTime"2026-06-15T18:00:00+02:00[Europe/Paris]"
//#
//# ny == paris // → True (same instant)
//# ```
//#
//# The offset or zone decides how a value displays and takes no part in
//# `==`. To ask for the same instant in the same zone, compare the displayed
//# forms too, since a `DateTime` displays with its zone bracket:
//#
//# ```nomi
//# a == b and Display.to_string(a) == Display.to_string(b)
//# ```
//#
//# `==` reaches these types' `Equatable` impls by the spec's rule for
//# operators on a type with a hand-written `Equatable`
//# ([Type relations](/spec/type-relations/)). `Date`, `Time` and
//# `NaiveDateTime` use structural equality (every field equal) and order
//# chronologically by field order.
//#
//# ## Text for a reader
//#
//# The calendar types write themselves for a reader in three ways,
//# Gregorian only: by a style (`format`), by a skeleton (`format_fields`),
//# and by a fixed numeric pattern (`to_string_with`, read back with
//# `parse_with`). `parse_in` reads what a reader typed. `Date`, `Time`,
//# `NaiveDateTime` and `DateTime` implement `Localize` with `format`'s
//# defaults; `Display` stays ISO 8601.
//#
//# **Styles.** `DateStyle` and `TimeStyle` are `Full`, `Long`, `Medium` and
//# `Short`, and the locale decides the rest. A date and a time join as the
//# locale's date-time pattern for the date's style joins them. A `Long`
//# time of a `DateTime` ends with its offset in the locale's short GMT
//# format (`GMT+2`, `UTC+2` in `fr`) and a `Full` one in its long format
//# (`GMT+02:00`); std has no zone names. A `Time` and a `NaiveDateTime`
//# have no zone, so their `Full` and `Long` times write what `Medium` does.
//#
//# **Skeletons.** `DateFields`, `TimeFields` and `DateTimeFields` name the
//# fields a value shows and their widths as a CLDR skeleton: `y` the year,
//# `M` or `L` the month (`M` its number, `MMM` its short name, `MMMM` its
//# name), `d` the day, `E` the weekday, `G` the era, `j` the hour as the
//# locale writes it (`h` a 12-hour clock's, `H` a 24-hour one's), `m`, `s`
//# and `S`. Each locale's data gives the order, the punctuation and the
//# words: `DateFields"MMMMd"` is `October 20` in `en`, `20 października`
//# in `pl` and `10月20日` in `ja`. This is ICU's date-time pattern generator
//# over the locale's available formats (UTS #35, *Matching Skeletons*): the
//# available skeleton with the same fields and the nearest widths, widened
//# to the widths asked for, except a field the locale writes in another
//# form (`ja` writes a month as a number where a skeleton names it); a
//# skeleton with date and time fields that no one format covers is the two
//# joined. The literal is checked when the program is checked: a letter
//# that is no field, a field the type does not hold (an hour in a
//# `DateFields`), a field named twice or one too wide is a compile error at
//# the literal. When every skeleton a built program uses is a literal, the
//# build resolves each one for each listed locale, and the image carries
//# those patterns instead of the locales' available formats.
//#
//# **Fixed patterns.** `DatePattern`, `TimePattern` and `DateTimePattern`
//# are numeric LDML patterns that write the same text in every locale, for
//# file names, logs and protocols: `y` (`yy` two digits), `M`, `d`, `D`,
//# `H`, `m`, `s` (each doubled for two digits), `S` (one per fraction
//# digit), and quoted or non-letter text. A month or weekday name, AM or
//# PM, an era and a zone are words or need them, so a pattern holds none:
//# ``typed literal DatePattern"dd MMM yyyy" is invalid: invalid format:
//# `MMM` writes the month's name, which is a word of a language: a fixed
//# pattern writes numbers, the same in every locale; write it for a reader
//# with a skeleton and a locale, as Date.format_fields(date, locale,
//# DateFields"yMMMd")``. `parse_with` reads what `to_string_with` writes, a
//# one-digit field standing for a two-digit one. `DatePattern.parse` and its
//# kin check a pattern made while the program runs.
//#
//# **Parsing.** `parse_in` reads text by the locale's pattern at a style,
//# strictly by default: a two-digit field may be written with one digit,
//# any space stands for a space the locale writes, and ASCII digits for the
//# locale's. Lenient parsing also reads names in any width or case, with or
//# without their final period, any spaces and punctuation between fields, a
//# four-digit year where the locale writes two, and spaces around the text.
//# A two-digit year is 2000 plus its digits. Text that does not read is
//# `Error.InvalidFormat`, naming what was expected where, and a value that
//# is no date is `Error.InvalidValue` (`month 2 of 2026 has no day 31`).
//#
//# ## Embedded IANA tzdata
//#
//# `std::calendar` carries the IANA time zone database, so zone lookups
//# (`America/New_York`, `Asia/Tokyo`, ...) work on every host without
//# system-provided tzdata. Programs ship with their own zone database, and
//# behavior is the same on every operating system and in minimal Docker
//# images.

import
    add.Add
    bool.Bool.{self, False, True}
    comparable.{Comparable, Ordering}
    debug.Debug
    display.Display
    duration.Duration
    equatable.Equatable
    hashable.Hashable
    instant.Instant
    int.{Int, Int64, NonZeroInt, PositiveInt}
    iter.Iter
    lists.List
    literals.{Fragment, Literal}
    locale.Locale
    localize.Localize
    option.Option.{None, Some}
    results.Result.{self, Err, Ok}
    strings.String
    subtract.Subtract
    unit.Unit
end

/// What went wrong building, parsing or converting a calendar value. Each
/// variant holds the reason as text, and `Display` writes the kind and then
/// the reason.
///
/// Every type's constructors and parsers return `InvalidFormat`, for text
/// that does not read, and `InvalidValue`, for a value that is no date or
/// time (`day 30 not valid for 2026-02`). The other three come from
/// `DateTime` alone. `UnknownZone` is a zone name the embedded zone
/// database lacks, or a machine zone with no IANA name
/// (`DateTime.local_zone`). `Nonexistent` is a wall reading in a
/// spring-forward gap and `Ambiguous` one in a fall-back fold, which
/// `DateTime.in_zone` returns only with `Disambiguation.Reject`; the other
/// modes resolve both.
//! assert Date.new(2026, 2, 30) == Err(Error.InvalidValue("day 30 not valid for 2026-02"))
//!
//! assert Display.to_string(Error.InvalidValue("month out of range: 13"))
//!     == "invalid value: month out of range: 13"
//
pub enum Error
    InvalidFormat String
    InvalidValue String
    UnknownZone String
    Nonexistent String
    Ambiguous String
end

derive Equatable for Error

impl Display for Error
    fn to_string(e: Error) -> String
        case e
            Error.InvalidFormat(s) -> "invalid format: " + s
            Error.InvalidValue(s)  -> "invalid value: " + s
            Error.UnknownZone(s)   -> "unknown zone: " + s
            Error.Nonexistent(s)   -> "nonexistent local time: " + s
            Error.Ambiguous(s)     -> "ambiguous local time: " + s
        end
    end
end

// ============================================================================
// Civil Periods
// ============================================================================
/// Whole calendar years for civil calendar arithmetic on date-bearing values.
/// Adding years to February 29 gives February 28 in a year that is not a
/// leap year.
//! assert Date"2024-02-29" + Years(1) == Date"2025-02-28"
//! assert Date"2024-02-29" + Years(4) == Date"2028-02-29"
//
pub type Years Int

derive Equatable for Years
derive Hashable for Years
derive Comparable for Years
derive Display for Years

/// Whole calendar months for civil calendar arithmetic on date-bearing values.
/// Adding months clamps to the end of the month.
//! assert Date"2026-01-31" + Months(1) == Date"2026-02-28"
//
pub type Months Int

derive Equatable for Months
derive Hashable for Months
derive Comparable for Months
derive Display for Months

/// Whole calendar weeks for civil calendar arithmetic on date-bearing values.
//! assert Date"2026-05-04" + Weeks(2) == Date"2026-05-18"
//
pub type Weeks Int

derive Equatable for Weeks
derive Hashable for Weeks
derive Comparable for Weeks
derive Display for Weeks

/// Whole calendar days for civil calendar arithmetic on date-bearing values.
/// In a zone, a day keeps the time of day across a daylight-saving change.
//! assert Date"2026-05-04" - Days(4) == Date"2026-04-30"
//!
//! assert DateTime"2026-03-07T12:00:00-05:00[America/New_York]" + Days(1)
//!     == DateTime"2026-03-08T12:00:00-04:00[America/New_York]"
//
pub type Days Int

derive Equatable for Days
derive Hashable for Days
derive Comparable for Days
derive Display for Days

/// Whole civil hours for date-time wall-clock arithmetic. They move the wall
/// reading, so across a spring-forward transition `+ Hours(24)` keeps the
/// time of day where `+ Duration.hours(24)` moves it.
//! start = DateTime"2026-03-07T12:00:00-05:00[America/New_York]"
//! assert start + Hours(24) == DateTime"2026-03-08T12:00:00-04:00[America/New_York]"
//! assert start + Duration.hours(24) == DateTime"2026-03-08T13:00:00-04:00[America/New_York]"
//
pub type Hours Int

derive Equatable for Hours
derive Hashable for Hours
derive Comparable for Hours
derive Display for Hours

/// Whole civil minutes for date-time wall-clock arithmetic.
pub type Minutes Int

derive Equatable for Minutes
derive Hashable for Minutes
derive Comparable for Minutes
derive Display for Minutes

/// Whole civil seconds for date-time wall-clock arithmetic.
pub type Seconds Int

derive Equatable for Seconds
derive Hashable for Seconds
derive Comparable for Seconds
derive Display for Seconds

/// Whole civil milliseconds for date-time wall-clock arithmetic.
pub type Milliseconds Int

derive Equatable for Milliseconds
derive Hashable for Milliseconds
derive Comparable for Milliseconds
derive Display for Milliseconds

/// Whole civil microseconds for date-time wall-clock arithmetic.
pub type Microseconds Int

derive Equatable for Microseconds
derive Hashable for Microseconds
derive Comparable for Microseconds
derive Display for Microseconds

/// Whole civil nanoseconds for date-time wall-clock arithmetic.
pub type Nanoseconds Int

derive Equatable for Nanoseconds
derive Hashable for Nanoseconds
derive Comparable for Nanoseconds
derive Display for Nanoseconds

/// The year, the month (1 to 12) and the day of the month of a value that
/// has a date. `Date`, `NaiveDateTime`, `OffsetDateTime` and `DateTime`
/// implement it; the last two read their wall clock at their offset or in
/// their zone.
pub interface DateParts
    fn year(value: self) -> Int
    fn month(value: self) -> Int
    fn day(value: self) -> Int
end

/// The hour (0 to 23), minute, second and nanosecond of a value that has a
/// time of day. `Time`, `NaiveDateTime`, `OffsetDateTime` and `DateTime`
/// implement it; the last two read their wall clock at their offset or in
/// their zone.
pub interface TimeParts
    fn hour(value: self) -> Int
    fn minute(value: self) -> Int
    fn second(value: self) -> Int
    fn nanosecond(value: self) -> Int
end

/// The `Instant` a value names and its UTC offset at that instant.
/// `OffsetDateTime` and `DateTime` implement it.
pub interface Anchored
    fn to_instant(value: self) -> Instant
    fn offset(value: self) -> Duration
end

/// A calendar date: a year, a month and a day, with no time of day and no
/// zone. Build one with the `Date"…"` typed literal, `Date.new(y, m, d)` or
/// `Date.parse`, and read its parts with `Date.year`, `Date.month` and
/// `Date.day` (the `DateParts` interface). The year is an `Int` of any
/// size; one outside 0000 to 9999 is written with a sign and at least six
/// digits.
//! assert Display.to_string(Date"2026-05-04") == "2026-05-04"
//! assert Date.month(Date"2026-05-04") == 5
//! assert Display.to_string(Date.new(-1, 12, 31)!) == "-000001-12-31"
//! assert Date"2026-05-04" + Years(300_000_000_000) == Date"+300000002026-05-04"
//
pub opaque struct Date
    year:  Int
    month: Int64
    day:   Int64
end

impl Date
    /// Construct a `Date`, checking that the month is 1 to 12 and has the
    /// day: no February 30, and February 29 only in a leap year. A date
    /// that does not exist is `Err(.InvalidValue(...))`. Any year is valid.
    //! assert Date.new(2026, 5, 4)? == Date"2026-05-04"
    //! assert Err(Error.InvalidValue(_msg)) = Date.new(2026, 2, 30)
    //! assert Date.new(Int.pow(10, 30), 2, 29)? == Date"+1000000000000000000000000000000-02-29"
    //
    pub fn new(year: Int, month: Int, day: Int) -> Result<Date, Error>
        attempt = date_new_raw(year, month, day)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    /// Parse an ISO 8601 date (`YYYY-MM-DD`). The `Date"…"` typed literal
    /// reads its text the same way. A year outside 0000 to 9999 takes a
    /// sign, as `Display` writes it; a year of more than four digits may
    /// go without one.
    //! assert Date.parse("2026-05-04")? == Date"2026-05-04"
    //! assert Err(Error.InvalidFormat(_msg)) = Date.parse("05/04/2026")
    //! assert Date.parse("-000044-03-15")? == Date.new(-44, 3, 15)?
    //! assert Date.parse("10000-01-01")? == Date"+010000-01-01"
    //
    pub fn parse(s: String) -> Result<Date, Error>
        attempt = date_parse_raw(s)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    /// The number of days from `earlier` to `later`: positive when `later`
    /// is the later date, negative when it is the earlier one.
    //! assert Date.days_between(Date"2026-05-04", Date"2026-05-07") == 3
    //! assert Date.days_between(Date"2026-05-07", Date"2026-05-04") == -3
    //
    pub host fn days_between(earlier: Date, later: Date) -> Int

    /// The day of the week the date falls on.
    //! assert Date.weekday(Date"2026-10-20") == Weekday.Tuesday
    //! assert Date.weekday(Date"2026-10-25") == Weekday.Sunday
    //! assert Date.weekday(Date"1969-12-29") == Weekday.Monday
    //
    pub fn weekday(d: Date) -> Weekday
        days = Date.days_between(Date{year: 1970, month: 1, day: 5}, d)

        case (days % 7 + 7) % 7
            0 -> Weekday.Monday
            1 -> Weekday.Tuesday
            2 -> Weekday.Wednesday
            3 -> Weekday.Thursday
            4 -> Weekday.Friday
            5 -> Weekday.Saturday
            _ -> Weekday.Sunday
        end
    end

    /// Whether `year` is a leap year in the Gregorian calendar, which
    /// `Date` follows for every year: one divisible by 4, except a century
    /// year not divisible by 400.
    //! assert Date.is_leap_year(2024)
    //! assert Date.is_leap_year(2000)
    //! refute Date.is_leap_year(1900)
    //! refute Date.is_leap_year(2026)
    //
    pub fn is_leap_year(year: Int) -> Bool
        year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0)
    end

    /// How many days month `month` (1 to 12) of `year` has: 28 to 31,
    /// with 29 in February of a leap year. A month outside 1 to 12 is
    /// `Err(.InvalidValue(...))`, as `Date.new` returns for it.
    //! assert Date.days_in_month(2026, 1) == Ok(31)
    //! assert Date.days_in_month(2026, 4) == Ok(30)
    //! assert Date.days_in_month(2026, 2) == Ok(28)
    //! assert Date.days_in_month(2024, 2) == Ok(29)
    //! assert Date.days_in_month(2026, 13) == Err(Error.InvalidValue("month out of range: 13"))
    //
    pub fn days_in_month(year: Int, month: Int) -> Result<PositiveInt, Error>
        case
            month < 1 or month > 12 -> Err(Error.InvalidValue("month out of range: ${month}"))
            month == 2 and Date.is_leap_year(year) -> Ok(29)
            month == 2 -> Ok(28)
            month == 4 or month == 6 or month == 9 or month == 11 -> Ok(30)
            _ -> Ok(31)
        end
    end

    /// The date as a reader of `locale` writes it, at the length `style`
    /// says: the locale decides the order, the punctuation and the words.
    //! d = Date"2026-10-20"
    //! assert Date.format(d, Locale"en") == "Oct 20, 2026"
    //! assert Date.format(d, Locale"en", style: .Full) == "Tuesday, October 20, 2026"
    //! assert Date.format(d, Locale"en", style: .Short) == "10/20/26"
    //! assert Date.format(d, Locale"pl", style: .Long) == "20 października 2026"
    //! assert Date.format(d, Locale"de", style: .Full) == "Dienstag, 20. Oktober 2026"
    //! assert Date.format(d, Locale"ja", style: .Long) == "2026年10月20日"
    //
    pub fn format(d: Date, locale: Locale, style = DateStyle.Medium) -> String
        civil_format_style(locale, d.year, Date.fields_of(d), "${DateStyle.name(style)}/-")
    end

    /// The date as a reader of `locale` writes the fields `fields` names,
    /// at their widths: a CLDR skeleton, from which the locale's data gives
    /// the order, the punctuation and the words. `yMMMd` is a year, a
    /// month's short name and a day; `MMMMEEEEd` a month's name, a weekday's
    /// and a day. A literal skeleton is checked when the program is checked.
    //! d = Date"2026-10-20"
    //! assert Date.format_fields(d, Locale"en", DateFields"yMMMd") == "Oct 20, 2026"
    //! assert Date.format_fields(d, Locale"pl", DateFields"yMMMd") == "20 paź 2026"
    //! assert Date.format_fields(d, Locale"en", DateFields"MMMMEEEEd") == "Tuesday, October 20"
    //! assert Date.format_fields(d, Locale"pl", DateFields"MMMMd") == "20 października"
    //! assert Date.format_fields(d, Locale"ja", DateFields"MMMMEEEEd") == "10月20日火曜日"
    //
    pub fn format_fields(d: Date, locale: Locale, fields: DateFields) -> String
        DateFields(skeleton) = fields
        civil_format_fields(locale, d.year, Date.fields_of(d), skeleton)
    end

    /// Read a date a reader of `locale` typed, as `Date.format` writes it at
    /// `style`, strictly unless `lenient: True`. Strict takes one digit
    /// for a two-digit day or month, any space for a space the locale
    /// writes, and ASCII digits for the locale's. Lenient also takes a name
    /// in any width or case, with or without its final period, any spaces
    /// and punctuation between the fields, a four-digit year where the
    /// locale writes two, and spaces around the date. A two-digit year is
    /// 2000 plus its digits, at every style.
    //! assert Date.parse_in("4.5.26", Locale"de") == Ok(Date"2026-05-04")
    //! assert Date.parse_in("20.10.26", Locale"de", style: .Medium) == Ok(Date"2026-10-20")
    //! assert Date.parse_in("10/20/26", Locale"en") == Ok(Date"2026-10-20")
    //! assert Date.parse_in("Oct 20, 2026", Locale"en", style: .Medium) == Ok(Date"2026-10-20")
    //!
    //! assert Date.parse_in("october 20 2026", Locale"en", style: .Medium, lenient: True)
    //!     == Ok(Date"2026-10-20")
    //!
    //! assert Err(Error.InvalidFormat(_why)) = Date.parse_in("4.5.2026", Locale"de")
    //! assert Date.parse_in("4.5.2026", Locale"de", lenient: True) == Ok(Date"2026-05-04")
    //!
    //! assert Date.parse_in("31.2.26", Locale"de")
    //!     == Err(Error.InvalidValue("month 2 of 2026 has no day 31"))
    //
    pub fn parse_in(
        text:    String,
        locale:  Locale,
        style:   DateStyle = DateStyle.Short,
        lenient: Bool = False,
    ) -> Result<Date, Error>
        attempt = date_parse_style(text, locale, "${DateStyle.name(style)}/-", lenient)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    /// The date by a fixed numeric pattern: the same text in every locale,
    /// for file names, logs and protocols.
    //! assert Date.to_string_with(Date"2026-10-19", DatePattern"yyyy-MM-dd") == "2026-10-19"
    //! assert Date.to_string_with(Date"2026-05-04", DatePattern"dd/MM/yyyy") == "04/05/2026"
    //! assert Date.to_string_with(Date"2026-05-04", DatePattern"yyyyMMdd") == "20260504"
    //
    pub fn to_string_with(d: Date, pattern: DatePattern) -> String
        DatePattern(p) = pattern
        civil_format_pattern(d.year, Date.fields_of(d), p)
    end

    /// Read a date by a fixed numeric pattern, as `Date.to_string_with`
    /// writes it. A one-digit day or month stands for a two-digit one.
    //! assert Date.parse_with("19/10/2026", DatePattern"dd/MM/yyyy") == Ok(Date"2026-10-19")
    //! assert Date.parse_with("20260504", DatePattern"yyyyMMdd") == Ok(Date"2026-05-04")
    //! assert Err(Error.InvalidFormat(_why)) = Date.parse_with("2026-10-19", DatePattern"dd/MM/yyyy")
    //
    pub fn parse_with(text: String, pattern: DatePattern) -> Result<Date, Error>
        DatePattern(p) = pattern
        attempt = date_parse_pattern(text, p)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    // The fields after the year a pattern writes a date by:
    // civil_format_style's list.
    fn fields_of(d: Date) -> List<Int64>
        [d.month, d.day, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1]
    end
end

derive Equatable for Date
derive Hashable for Date
derive Comparable for Date

impl Literal for Date
    fn from_fragments(fragments: List<Fragment<String>>) -> Result<Date, Error>
        body = Iter.reduce(fragments, "", fn acc, frag ->
            case frag
                .Static(s)  -> acc + s
                .Dynamic(v) -> acc + v
            end
        end)

        Date.parse(body)
    end
end

impl Display for Date
    /// ISO 8601 date (`YYYY-MM-DD`). A year outside 0000 to 9999 has a sign
    /// and at least six digits.
    host fn to_string(d: Date) -> String
end

impl Debug for Date
    fn inspect(d: Date) -> String
        "${d}"
    end
end

impl DateParts for Date
    fn year(value: Date) -> Int
        value.year
    end

    fn month(value: Date) -> Int
        value.month
    end

    fn day(value: Date) -> Int
        value.day
    end
end

impl Add<Years, Date> for Date
    /// Add whole calendar years, clamping leap days when needed.
    fn add(lhs: Date, Years(n)) -> Date
        date_add_years(lhs, n)
    end
end

impl Add<Months, Date> for Date
    /// Add whole calendar months, clamping end-of-month.
    //! assert Date"2011-01-31" + Months(1) == Date"2011-02-28"
    //! assert Date"2012-01-31" + Months(1) == Date"2012-02-29"
    //
    fn add(lhs: Date, Months(n)) -> Date
        date_add_months(lhs, n)
    end
end

impl Add<Weeks, Date> for Date
    /// Add whole calendar weeks.
    fn add(lhs: Date, Weeks(n)) -> Date
        lhs + Days(n * 7)
    end
end

impl Add<Days, Date> for Date
    /// Add whole calendar days.
    fn add(lhs: Date, Days(n)) -> Date
        date_add_days(lhs, n)
    end
end

impl Subtract<Years, Date> for Date
    /// Subtract whole calendar years, clamping leap days when needed.
    fn subtract(lhs: Date, Years(n)) -> Date
        lhs + Years(-n)
    end
end

impl Subtract<Months, Date> for Date
    /// Subtract whole calendar months, clamping end-of-month.
    fn subtract(lhs: Date, Months(n)) -> Date
        lhs + Months(-n)
    end
end

impl Subtract<Weeks, Date> for Date
    /// Subtract whole calendar weeks.
    fn subtract(lhs: Date, Weeks(n)) -> Date
        lhs + Weeks(-n)
    end
end

impl Subtract<Days, Date> for Date
    /// Subtract whole calendar days.
    fn subtract(lhs: Date, Days(n)) -> Date
        lhs + Days(-n)
    end
end

// A component its caller has checked is within its field's range, which an
// Int64 holds: the field's value.
fn component(n: Int) -> Int64
    case Int64.try_from(n)
        Some(checked) -> checked
        None          -> 0
    end
end

// ============================================================================
// Time
// ============================================================================
/// A time of day: an hour, a minute, a second and a nanosecond, with no
/// date and no zone. Build one with the `Time"…"` typed literal,
/// `Time.new(h, m, s, ns)` or `Time.parse`, and read its parts with
/// `Time.hour`, `Time.minute`, `Time.second` and `Time.nanosecond` (the
/// `TimeParts` interface).
//! assert Display.to_string(Time"08:30:00") == "08:30:00"
//! assert Time.hour(Time"08:30:00") == 8
//
pub opaque struct Time
    hour:       Int64
    minute:     Int64
    second:     Int64
    nanosecond: Int64
end

impl Time
    /// Construct a `Time`, checking each part's range: the hour 0 to 23,
    /// the minute and second 0 to 59, the nanosecond 0 to 999_999_999. One
    /// out of range is `Err(.InvalidValue(...))`.
    //! assert Time.new(8, 30)? == Time"08:30:00"
    //! assert Time.new(8, 30, 15, 250_000_000)? == Time"08:30:15.25"
    //! assert Err(Error.InvalidValue(_msg)) = Time.new(24, 0)
    //
    pub fn new(hour: Int, minute: Int, second: Int = 0, nanosecond: Int = 0) -> Result<Time, Error>
        if hour < 0 or hour > 23 then
            Err(Error.InvalidValue("hour out of range: " + Int.to_string(hour)))
        else if minute < 0 or minute > 59 then
            Err(Error.InvalidValue("minute out of range: " + Int.to_string(minute)))
        else if second < 0 or second > 59 then
            Err(Error.InvalidValue("second out of range: " + Int.to_string(second)))
        else if nanosecond < 0 or nanosecond > 999_999_999 then
            Err(Error.InvalidValue("nanosecond out of range: " + Int.to_string(nanosecond)))
        else
            Ok(
                Time{
                    hour:       component(hour),
                    minute:     component(minute),
                    second:     component(second),
                    nanosecond: component(nanosecond),
                }
            )
        end
    end

    /// Parse a time of day (`HH:MM:SS` or `HH:MM:SS.fff…`, with up to nine
    /// digits of the second). The seconds are required.
    //! assert Time.parse("08:30:00")? == Time"08:30:00"
    //! assert Err(Error.InvalidFormat(_msg)) = Time.parse("08:30")
    //! assert Err(Error.InvalidFormat(_msg)) = Time.parse("nope")
    //
    pub fn parse(s: String) -> Result<Time, Error>
        attempt = time_parse_raw(s)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    /// Exact duration from `earlier` to `later` within one day, negative
    /// when `later` is the earlier time.
    //! assert Time.between(Time"08:30:00", Time"08:33:00") == Duration.minutes(3)
    //! assert Time.between(Time"08:33:00", Time"08:30:00") == Duration.minutes(-3)
    //
    pub fn between(earlier: Time, later: Time) -> Duration
        Duration.nanoseconds(day_nanos(later) - day_nanos(earlier))
    end

    /// The time of day as a reader of `locale` writes it, at the length
    /// `style` says: a 12-hour clock with AM and PM in `en`, a 24-hour one
    /// in `de`. A `Time` has no zone, so `Full` and `Long` write what
    /// `Medium` does.
    //! t = Time"21:30:00"
    //! assert Time.format(t, Locale"en") == "9:30\u{202f}PM"
    //! assert Time.format(t, Locale"de") == "21:30"
    //! assert Time.format(t, Locale"fr", style: .Medium) == "21:30:00"
    //
    pub fn format(t: Time, locale: Locale, style = TimeStyle.Short) -> String
        civil_format_style(locale, 1970, Time.fields_of(t), "-/${TimeStyle.name(style)}")
    end

    /// The time of day as a reader of `locale` writes the fields `fields`
    /// names: `jm` is the hour as the locale writes it and the minutes, `Hm`
    /// a 24-hour clock, `hm` a 12-hour one.
    //! t = Time"21:30:00"
    //! assert Time.format_fields(t, Locale"en", TimeFields"jm") == "9:30\u{202f}PM"
    //! assert Time.format_fields(t, Locale"en", TimeFields"Hm") == "21:30"
    //! assert Time.format_fields(t, Locale"de", TimeFields"hm") == "9:30\u{202f}PM"
    //
    pub fn format_fields(t: Time, locale: Locale, fields: TimeFields) -> String
        TimeFields(skeleton) = fields
        civil_format_fields(locale, 1970, Time.fields_of(t), skeleton)
    end

    /// Read a time of day a reader of `locale` typed, as `Time.format`
    /// writes it at `style`, strictly unless `lenient: True`, as
    /// `Date.parse_in` reads a date.
    //! assert Time.parse_in("9:30\u{202f}PM", Locale"en") == Ok(Time"21:30:00")
    //! assert Time.parse_in("9:30 PM", Locale"en") == Ok(Time"21:30:00")
    //! assert Time.parse_in("9:30 pm", Locale"en", lenient: True) == Ok(Time"21:30:00")
    //! assert Time.parse_in("21:30", Locale"de") == Ok(Time"21:30:00")
    //
    pub fn parse_in(
        text:    String,
        locale:  Locale,
        style:   TimeStyle = TimeStyle.Short,
        lenient: Bool = False,
    ) -> Result<Time, Error>
        attempt = time_parse_style(text, locale, "-/${TimeStyle.name(style)}", lenient)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    /// The time of day by a fixed numeric pattern, a 24-hour clock.
    //! assert Time.to_string_with(Time"09:05:00", TimePattern"HHmmss") == "090500"
    //! assert Time.to_string_with(Time"09:05:00.25", TimePattern"HH:mm:ss.SSS") == "09:05:00.250"
    //
    pub fn to_string_with(t: Time, pattern: TimePattern) -> String
        TimePattern(p) = pattern
        civil_format_pattern(1970, Time.fields_of(t), p)
    end

    /// Read a time of day by a fixed numeric pattern.
    //! assert Time.parse_with("0905", TimePattern"HHmm") == Ok(Time"09:05:00")
    //
    pub fn parse_with(text: String, pattern: TimePattern) -> Result<Time, Error>
        TimePattern(p) = pattern
        attempt = time_parse_pattern(text, p)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    // The fields after the year a pattern writes a time by.
    fn fields_of(t: Time) -> List<Int64>
        [1, 1, t.hour, t.minute, t.second, t.nanosecond, 0, 2]
    end
end

/// Total nanoseconds since midnight, for `Time.between`.
//! assert Time"08:33:00" |> day_nanos() == 30780000000000
//
fn day_nanos(t: Time) -> Int
    t.hour * 3_600_000_000_000 + t.minute * 60_000_000_000 + t.second * 1_000_000_000 + t.nanosecond
end

derive Equatable for Time
derive Hashable for Time
derive Comparable for Time

impl Literal for Time
    fn from_fragments(fragments: List<Fragment<String>>) -> Result<Time, Error>
        body = Iter.reduce(fragments, "", fn acc, frag ->
            case frag
                .Static(s)  -> acc + s
                .Dynamic(v) -> acc + v
            end
        end)

        Time.parse(body)
    end
end

impl Display for Time
    /// ISO 8601 time (`HH:MM:SS`). A fraction of a second follows only
    /// when it is not zero, without trailing zeros: `09:30:00`,
    /// `09:30:00.25`.
    host fn to_string(t: Time) -> String
end

impl Debug for Time
    fn inspect(t: Time) -> String
        "${t}"
    end
end

impl TimeParts for Time
    fn hour(value: Time) -> Int
        value.hour
    end

    fn minute(value: Time) -> Int
        value.minute
    end

    fn second(value: Time) -> Int
        value.second
    end

    fn nanosecond(value: Time) -> Int
        value.nanosecond
    end
end

impl Add<Duration, Time> for Time
    /// Add an exact `Duration`, wrapping around midnight.
    //! assert Time"08:33:00" + Duration.minutes(3) == Time"08:36:00"
    //! assert Time"23:00:00" + Duration.hours(2) == Time"01:00:00"
    //
    fn add(lhs: Time, rhs: Duration) -> Time
        time_add_nanos(lhs, Int.modulo(Duration.as_nanos(rhs), 86_400_000_000_000))
    end
end

impl Subtract<Duration, Time> for Time
    /// Subtract an exact `Duration`, wrapping around midnight.
    //! assert Time"08:36:00" - Duration.minutes(3) == Time"08:33:00"
    //
    fn subtract(lhs: Time, rhs: Duration) -> Time
        lhs + rhs * -1
    end
end

// ============================================================================
// NaiveDateTime
// ============================================================================
/// A date and a time of day with no zone or offset: a wall-clock reading
/// such as `2026-05-04T14:30:00`. It looks like a timestamp, but without a
/// zone it names no moment. Use `DateTime` for a moment, and
/// `NaiveDateTime` where the value is a wall reading: a calendar template,
/// a schedule pattern, a local time that a zone resolves later.
//! dt = NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T14:30:00"
//! assert dt == NaiveDateTime.of(Date"2026-05-04", Time"14:30:00")
//! assert Display.to_string(dt + Hours(10)) == "2026-05-05T00:30:00"
//
pub opaque struct NaiveDateTime
    year:       Int
    month:      Int64
    day:        Int64
    hour:       Int64
    minute:     Int64
    second:     Int64
    nanosecond: Int64
end

impl NaiveDateTime
    /// The date and time as a reader of `locale` writes them, the date at
    /// the length `date` says and the time at `time`'s, joined as the
    /// locale joins them.
    //! dt = NaiveDateTime"2026-10-20T09:30:00"
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.format(dt, Locale"en") == "Oct 20, 2026, 9:30\u{202f}AM"
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.format(dt, Locale"de", date: .Long) == "20. Oktober 2026, 09:30"
    //
    pub fn format(
        dt:     NaiveDateTime,
        locale: Locale,
        date:   DateStyle = DateStyle.Medium,
        time:   TimeStyle = TimeStyle.Short,
    ) -> String
        civil_format_style(
            locale,
            dt.year,
            NaiveDateTime.fields_of(dt),
            "${DateStyle.name(date)}/${TimeStyle.name(time)}",
        )
    end

    /// The date and time as a reader of `locale` writes the fields `fields`
    /// names.
    //! dt = NaiveDateTime"2026-10-20T09:30:00"
    //!
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.format_fields(dt, Locale"en", DateTimeFields"MMMdjm")
    //!     == "Oct 20, 9:30\u{202f}AM"
    //
    pub fn format_fields(dt: NaiveDateTime, locale: Locale, fields: DateTimeFields) -> String
        DateTimeFields(skeleton) = fields
        civil_format_fields(locale, dt.year, NaiveDateTime.fields_of(dt), skeleton)
    end

    /// The date and time by a fixed numeric pattern.
    //! dt = NaiveDateTime"2026-10-19T09:30:00"
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.to_string_with(dt, DateTimePattern"yyyyMMdd'T'HHmmss") == "20261019T093000"
    //
    pub fn to_string_with(dt: NaiveDateTime, pattern: DateTimePattern) -> String
        DateTimePattern(p) = pattern
        civil_format_pattern(dt.year, NaiveDateTime.fields_of(dt), p)
    end

    /// Read a date and time by a fixed numeric pattern.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.parse_with("20261019T093000", DateTimePattern"yyyyMMdd'T'HHmmss")
    //!     == Ok(NaiveDateTime"2026-10-19T09:30:00")
    //
    pub fn parse_with(text: String, pattern: DateTimePattern) -> Result<NaiveDateTime, Error>
        DateTimePattern(p) = pattern
        attempt = naive_parse_pattern(text, p)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    // The fields after the year a pattern writes a date and time by.
    fn fields_of(dt: NaiveDateTime) -> List<Int64>
        [dt.month, dt.day, dt.hour, dt.minute, dt.second, dt.nanosecond, 0, 3]
    end

    /// Combine a `Date` and a `Time`. Both parts are valid already, so it
    /// cannot fail.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.of(Date"2026-05-04", Time"14:30:00") == NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T14:30:00"
    //
    pub fn of(d: Date, t: Time) -> NaiveDateTime
        NaiveDateTime{
            year:       d.year,
            month:      d.month,
            day:        d.day,
            hour:       t.hour,
            minute:     t.minute,
            second:     t.second,
            nanosecond: t.nanosecond,
        }
    end

    /// A `NaiveDateTime` at midnight (00:00:00) on the given date.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.at_midnight(Date"2026-05-04") == NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T00:00:00"
    //
    pub fn at_midnight(d: Date) -> NaiveDateTime
        NaiveDateTime{
            year:       d.year,
            month:      d.month,
            day:        d.day,
            hour:       0,
            minute:     0,
            second:     0,
            nanosecond: 0,
        }
    end

    /// The date.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.to_date(NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T14:30:00") == Date"2026-05-04"
    //
    pub host fn to_date(dt: NaiveDateTime) -> Date

    /// The time of day.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.to_time(NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T14:30:00") == Time"14:30:00"
    //
    pub host fn to_time(dt: NaiveDateTime) -> Time

    /// Construct from a year, month, day, hour and minute, and a second
    /// and nanosecond when given, checking each part's range and that the
    /// month has the day, as `Date.new` and `Time.new` do.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.new(2026, 5, 4, 14, 30)? == NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T14:30:00"
    //! assert Err(Error.InvalidValue(_msg)) = NaiveDateTime.new(2026, 2, 30, 12, 0)
    //
    pub fn new(
        year:       Int,
        month:      Int,
        day:        Int,
        hour:       Int,
        minute:     Int,
        second:     Int = 0,
        nanosecond: Int = 0,
    ) -> Result<NaiveDateTime, Error>
        NaiveDateTime.new_exact(year, month, day, hour, minute, second, nanosecond)
    end

    // The seven-component constructor, with no defaults of its own.
    //
    // The split exists so that `second: Int = 0` above has exactly ONE owner.
    // The Go half it reaches takes seven arguments and pads nothing, so a
    // default can only be written on a Nomi declaration, where the IR builder
    // reads it. Two layers, one encoding.
    //
    // `DateTime.in_zone` over `in_zone_resolve` below is this shape already.
    fn new_exact(
        year:       Int,
        month:      Int,
        day:        Int,
        hour:       Int,
        minute:     Int,
        second:     Int,
        nanosecond: Int,
    ) -> Result<NaiveDateTime, Error>
        attempt = naive_new_exact_raw(year, month, day, hour, minute, second, nanosecond)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    /// Parse an ISO 8601 date and time with no offset
    /// (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS[.fff…]`). One space may stand for the `T`.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.parse("2026-05-04T14:30:00")?
    //!     == NaiveDateTime.of(Date"2026-05-04", Time"14:30:00")
    //!
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.parse("2026-05-04 14:30:00")? == NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T14:30:00"
    //
    pub fn parse(s: String) -> Result<NaiveDateTime, Error>
        attempt = naive_parse_raw(s)

        if attempt.kind == 0 then
            Ok(attempt.value)
        else
            Err(Error.of(attempt.kind, attempt.reason))
        end
    end

    /// Exact duration from `earlier` to `later`, counting each day as 24
    /// hours; negative when `later` is the earlier reading.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.between(NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T09:00:00", NaiveDateTime"2026-05-04T11:30:00")
    //!     == Duration.minutes(150)
    //
    pub fn between(earlier: NaiveDateTime, later: NaiveDateTime) -> Duration
        Duration.nanoseconds(naive_between_nanos(earlier, later))
    end

    /// The wall reading of an `Instant` at a fixed UTC `offset`, UTC when
    /// no offset is given: what a clock at that offset showed at that
    /// moment. A sub-second offset is truncated to whole seconds, as
    /// `OffsetDateTime.from_instant` truncates it, and an offset of any
    /// length moves the reading by that much. For a reading in an IANA
    /// zone, read `DateTime.from_instant_in(i, zone)` with
    /// `DateTime.to_naive`.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.from_instant(Instant.from_seconds(0)) == NaiveDateTime"1970-01-01T00:00:00"
    //!
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.from_instant(Instant.from_seconds(0), Duration.hours(-5))
    //!     == NaiveDateTime"1969-12-31T19:00:00"
    //!
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.from_instant(Instant.from_seconds(0), Duration.hours(48))
    //!     == NaiveDateTime"1970-01-03T00:00:00"
    //
    pub fn from_instant(i: Instant, offset: Duration = Duration.seconds(0)) -> NaiveDateTime
        epoch = NaiveDateTime{
            year:       1970,
            month:      1,
            day:        1,
            hour:       0,
            minute:     0,
            second:     0,
            nanosecond: 0,
        }

        epoch + Duration.nanoseconds(Instant.as_nanos(i)) + Duration.seconds(
            Duration.as_seconds(offset)
        )
    end

    /// Read the current wall clock in UTC: the form a database `TIMESTAMP`
    /// column (one without a time zone) holds when the program writes UTC
    /// into it. The same as `NaiveDateTime.from_instant(Instant.now())`. A
    /// naive reading names no moment by itself; for an absolute time read
    /// `Instant.now()`, and for a reading in a zone `DateTime.now_in`.
    //! before = NaiveDateTime.from_instant(Instant.now())
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.between(before, NaiveDateTime.utc_now()) >= Duration.seconds(0)
    //
    pub fn utc_now() -> NaiveDateTime
        NaiveDateTime.from_instant(Instant.now())
    end

    /// The `Instant` a clock at a fixed UTC `offset` showed this reading
    /// at, reading it as UTC when no offset is given: the inverse of
    /// `NaiveDateTime.from_instant`. An Instant has no range, so every
    /// reading has one. To resolve a reading in an IANA zone, use
    /// `DateTime.in_zone`.
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.to_instant(NaiveDateTime"1970-01-01T00:00:42") == Instant.from_seconds(42)
    //!
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.to_instant(NaiveDateTime"1969-12-31T19:00:00", Duration.hours(-5))
    //!     == Instant.from_seconds(0)
    //!
    //! assert Instant.to_seconds(NaiveDateTime.to_instant(NaiveDateTime"2300-01-01T00:00:00"))
    //!     == 10_413_792_000
    //!
    //! i = Instant.from_nanos(-1_700_000_000_123_456_789)
    //!
    //! assert NaiveDateTime.to_instant(NaiveDateTime.from_instant(i, Duration.hours(9)), Duration.hours(9))
    //!     == i
    //
    pub fn to_instant(dt: NaiveDateTime, offset: Duration = Duration.seconds(0)) -> Instant
        days = Date.days_between(Date{year: 1970, month: 1, day: 1}, NaiveDateTime.to_date(dt))
        seconds = ((days * 24 + dt.hour) * 60 + dt.minute) * 60 + dt.second
        Instant.from_nanos(seconds * 1_000_000_000 + dt.nanosecond - Duration.as_nanos(offset))
    end
end

derive Equatable for NaiveDateTime
derive Hashable for NaiveDateTime
derive Comparable for NaiveDateTime

impl Literal for NaiveDateTime
    fn from_fragments(fragments: List<Fragment<String>>) -> Result<NaiveDateTime, Error>
        body = Iter.reduce(fragments, "", fn acc, frag ->
            case frag
                .Static(s)  -> acc + s
                .Dynamic(v) -> acc + v
            end
        end)

        NaiveDateTime.parse(body)
    end
end

